      "filename": "Hammond RT-3 & Challen piano, Abbey Road Studios, 80th Anniversary, March 9, 2012.jpg",
      "title": "File:Hammond RT-3 & Challen piano, Abbey Road Studios, 80th Anniversary, March 9, 2012.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0e/Hammond_RT-3_%26_Challen_piano%2C_Abbey_Road_Studios%2C_80th_Anniversary%2C_March_9%2C_2012.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hammond_RT-3_%26_Challen_piano,_Abbey_Road_Studios,_80th_Anniversary,_March_9,_2012.jpg",
      "width": 4976,
      "height": 2800,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "MrJamesAckerley",
      "description": "Abbey Road Studios opens its doors to the public, 80th Anniversary, March 9, 2012[1]\n\nHammond RT-3 organ\n...\n\n\nChallen piano\nOne of two Challen pianos at Abbey Road Studios, these\ngive a nice warm tone; a softer sound than a ground piano or\nthe Steinway Vertegrand. Songwriters were especially found of \nsinging on pop songs. The pianos also had a mechanical \noption for a metalic sound, called a 'jangle box' setting.\nTis added brass strips over the strings, producing a sharp\nbright sound some musicians referred to as 'tack piano'.\nThis piano, built in the 1930's, has been used constantly in\nthe studios and was another favorite of the The Beatles,\nwho used it extensively on \"Magical Mystery Tour\" and \"The\n\nWhite Album\". It is still used on pop and rock sessions today.",
      "dateCreated": "2012-03-09 17:35:23",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "abbey-road-studios-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "gcpv",
      "mediumPath": "_m/g/c/p/v/abbey-road-studios-wp/hammond-rt-3-challen-piano-abb-medium.webp",
      "smallPath": "_m/g/c/p/v/abbey-road-studios-wp/hammond-rt-3-challen-piano-abb-small.webp"
    },
    {
